ay I went out for exercise in  the morning my heart rate's slightly  elevated all perfectly normal sit down  for lunch part way through lunch I felt  funny check and realized something is  going on so I I have the raw data for  the electric for the atrial fibrillation  but I don't have the expertise to do  anything with that that's just beyond me  but what I was able to do was to take  the background heart rate information  and to put the atrial fibrillation  episode in the context of the rest of  the day and there is some slight  relevance to to the when I was  discussing with this with the doctor  because what I could show was that  within a minute or two of after the  episode started there's an indication  there that shows I was doing  electrocardiogram so I could tell this  is evidence that I knew when I went into  atrial fibrillation sometimes people are  have signed don't know they're a atrial  fibrillation but I knew and and you can  also see that I came out of it uh  before they hooked me up to an  electrocardiogram in the emergency room  uh it actually goes on and off like  throwing a light switch so there was  none of these things I'm showing you are  have dramatic effects on my medical  treatment uh it's but in this particular  case there was some slight advantage in  having a discussion about whether I  needed to go in blood thinners and  whatever  um but the general point is is that this  stuff shows you a picture of your life  there's a lot of data there if you are  have just looked at Palmer Penguins one  too many times and you want to try  something else with deep hire you got  you may have you know hundreds of  thousands millions of rows of data that  you may find more interesting than  Palmer penguins so I've done a series of  blog posts about this if you have more  interest in figuring out how to do this  there's a lot more information there and  there's also a url url with an example  of some other things that people have  done uh with their data